{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/695478-what-supplements-do-you-recommend","id":695478,"title":"What supplements do you recommend?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-10-09T17:09:49-07:00","id":695478,"title":"What supplements do you recommend?","content":"I'm looking into getting a few supplements and would like to know if anyone has any recommendations. I thought I had read about a strix chronicle book with a whole adventure premade but the one I found on the drive thru rpg site seems to be fiction for mood or backstory.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8152-xizzicks","name":"Xizzicks","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8152_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"37"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-09T17:35:57-07:00","id":695514,"title":"","content":"There's a fiction anthology about the Strix, and then there's the Strix chronicle. I believe that Blood and Smoke (the Strix chronicle) is included entirely in Vampire: The Requiem 2e - so if you have 2e you have everything.<br /> <br /> I've seen recommendations elsewhere in this forum for Damnation City and Blood Sorcery as being workable with 2e.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-09T18:13:47-07:00","id":695562,"title":"","content":"<i>The Strix Chronicle Anthology</i> is a fiction collection. Second Edition was originally called <i>Blood and Smoke: The Strix Chronicle</i>, but never had adventures. The only changes between B&amp;S and VtR2 were some typo fixes, a new index, a new cover, and a small layout correction.<br /> <br /> I personally recommend <i>Damnation City</i>, the clanbooks, and <i>Blood Sorcery</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Cavaliers of Mars Creator</b><br /> <b>Retired CofD Lead</b><br /> <br /> Check out my <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Rose%20Bailey\" target=\"_blank\">guides to </a><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/261142/BITE-ME-How-to-Write-Vampire?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> and my <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse/pub/4895/Fantasy-Heartbreaker?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">indie games</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2-rose-bailey","name":"Rose Bailey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2361"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-09T18:13:54-07:00","id":695563,"title":"","content":"The book originally titled Blood &amp; Smoke: The Strix Chronicle was rebranded with a new cover as the Vampire: the Requiem Second Edition corebook. (The Strix Chronicle Anthology is a separate book, that's the short story collection.) It's still got all the adventure hooks, domain layouts and antagonist writeups the original had, but they're not quite a full premade adventure.<br /> <br /> Reap the Whirlwind, the second edition quickstart, does have a premade adventure for 2e VtR. 1e VtR has several short adventure writeups in the SAS format, and a few other 1e supplements have prewritten adventure material, including Requiem for Rome/Fall of the Camarilla and The Danse Macabre. It shouldn't be too difficult to use adventure material from one edition in the other edition; the core concepts are mostly close enough.<br /> <br /> Damnation City is a one-stop shop for various optional systems, example writeups and walkthroughs on building a city domain to run your game in, and developing stories and hooks in that domain.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-09T18:26:55-07:00","id":695574,"title":"","content":"<b>Vampire: The Requiem Second Edition</b> was originally released as <b>Blood And Smoke: The Strix Chronicle</b> - it includes example settings and antagonists, including a chapter on the Strix featuring examples ready to use, but it isn't an adventure book. <b>The Strix Chronicle Anthology</b> is a collection of fiction.<br /> <br /> There are <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse/pub/1/White-Wolf/subcategory/1_3848/Storytelling-Adventure-System\" target=\"_blank\">a number of published adventures available</a>, mostly designed for the first edition rules, although the free quickstart <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/114063/Reap-the-Whirlwind\" target=\"_blank\">Reap The Whirlwind</a> updates the adventure &quot;Into The Void&quot; for Second Edition. There's also <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/1459/Vampire-The-Requiem-Demo-Full\" target=\"_blank\">a multi-part adventure</a> in the free first edition demo.<br /> <br /> If I were to recommend one supplement, it would be <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/28796/Damnation-City\" target=\"_blank\">Damnation City</a>, the guide to creating urban chronicles, with enough examples that you can use it as a city sourcebook and collection of adventure hooks as well. It's great for urban-centred games beyond <b>Vampire</b> too. See the free <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/50756/Damnation-City-District-Map-Segments\" target=\"_blank\">map segments</a> supplement as well.<br /> <br /> The Clanbooks are also excellent, mostly in-setting information and inspirational fiction, while <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/88846/The-Danse-Macabre\" target=\"_blank\">The Danse Macabre</a> was designed as a rules and setting expansion for the first edition that saw early examples of various Second Edition rules, but also contains new covenants, advice on playing at different tiers of influence,a nd alternate playstyles.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Craig Oxbrow<br /> <b>The Trinity Continuum</b> freelancer","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/221-craig-oxbrow","name":"Craig Oxbrow","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar221_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"608"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-10T15:13:24-07:00","id":696540,"title":"","content":"Danse Macabre is the best book in the VtR line by miles.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-10T15:49:57-07:00","id":696577,"title":"","content":"I think it's worth mentioning, the DriveThruRPG copy of Damnation City is not very good. It's not the end of the world, but it makes reading it a bit of pain.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-12T03:18:02-07:00","id":698473,"title":"","content":"Damnation City can't be recommended enough.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MAKE LOVE, NOT SPAM!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1956-the-boz","name":"The Boz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1956_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"132"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-12T12:04:50-07:00","id":698859,"title":"","content":"I'd throw my voice behind the aforementioned Damnation City and Danse Macabre. I would also recommend:<br /> <br /> Mythologies: A collection of, well, myths. Some about the origins of vampires, some about facets of the vampire condition, some about monsters for vampires to fear.<br /> <br /> The Invictus and Ordo Dracul covenant books (they're the best at showing the covenants as organically evolving groups with all the attendant idiosyncrasies).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-12T14:50:27-07:00","id":698989,"title":"","content":"It's already been backed up enough, but I'll totally reinforce the utility of Damnation City. It's not just a great VtR supplement but also an all-around fantastic toolkit for any modern day game. I'm planning a game taking place in my hometown, and while I know the city like the back of my hand, DC provides plenty of on point advice to translating that raw information into narrative gold. Bits and pieces of it are a bit crunchy for my tastes, but luckily you can take everything a la carte. The writing is also well beyond what I'd expect from a typical RPG supplement. A lot of it, not even just the little interstitial fiction segments, borders on legit literary prose.<br /> <br /> I haven't dug too deep into the game's catalog (I've been wary for concern out of how bits and pieces convert to 2E and have plenty of material with the core and DC), but it seems pretty well to stand on its own. I've browsed through a couple of the covenant books and they seem pretty solid. I'd suggest picking one or two up if particular covs are going to have a particularly salient impact on your game. All-in-all, the game seems pretty modular and the writing seems solid altogether, so I suspect it's more about picking the books that fit your particular setting and mood rather than there being a de facto list of essentials.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-08T07:16:11-08:00","id":782287,"title":"","content":"Are the Covenant Books still relevant after the release of 2nd ed.?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":10022,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10022-kalamaris","user":"Kalamaris","date":". 12-08-2015, 10:18 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10022-kalamaris","name":"Kalamaris","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10022_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-08T16:38:36-08:00","id":782549,"title":"","content":"Not really. Most of the mechanics are obsolete or were transported to the default 2nd edition rulebook and the thematic demi roleplaying elements don't really apply since the covenants by default, don't fulfill the same roles they did in 1st edition anymore. You can certainly upgrade mechanics or cut and paste themes but why throw away money when they're in the process of making a 2nd edition book for them already","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5466-tsusasi","name":"tsusasi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5466_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1028"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-09T06:14:27-08:00","id":782753,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>tsusasi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/695478-what-supplements-do-you-recommend?p=782549#post782549\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not really. Most of the mechanics are obsolete or were transported to the default 2nd edition rulebook and the thematic demi roleplaying elements don't really apply since the covenants by default, don't fulfill the same roles they did in 1st edition anymore. You can certainly upgrade mechanics or cut and paste themes but why throw away money when they're in the process of making a 2nd edition book for them already</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for the reply. You see I'm in the (un)fortunate position to have many of them already.<br /> I own the Invictus, Ordo Dracul, Carthians, Lansea Sanctum, Circle of the Crone and VII books.<br /> Never got to read them before but they sit in my bookshelf patiently and I was planning to get to them after reading VtR 2nd ed. (order is on the way).<br /> <br /> I'm more interested in the fluff on the Covenants but I don't want to read obsolete material, so which of the above are the less affected by 2nd ed. changes fluff wise?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":10022,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10022-kalamaris","user":"Kalamaris","date":". 12-09-2015, 09:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10022-kalamaris","name":"Kalamaris","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10022_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-10T05:44:59-08:00","id":783393,"title":"","content":"For 2e? Actually, my recommendations are based entirely on what you, as an ST, would need. I don't actually think any of them are really useful for players. <br /> <br /> The clan books are primarily about stories behind the clan. While interesting reads, I don't think my players actually found the books impacted their actual play in any way, and they're more interested in moving forwards with their own views. Especially the Daeva - with their clan bane so different, they actually come off as a wildly different clan now. The Mehket clan book was all about the secret cults, and a character suffering from the Hollow condition, but the cults are primarily an Ordo Dracul thing now, and Hollow isn't a thing so much as the accumulation of occult banes. Ventrue madness is no longer a thing either. Actually, now that I think about it, I feel like the clan books might be slightly outdated already.<br /> <br /> Requiem for Rome and the clan books helped redefine the game from the 1e core book, but the 2e core book went beyond what was covered in what came before. Same thing with the Blasphemies and Sacraments book - its the halfway point between 1e and 2e for Theban and Cruac. Danse Macabre and Ancient Mysteries/Bloodlines is another in that same vein - a book with lots of lore for pushing 1e towards its new shiny 2e world. <br /> <br /> All the above are great for mining ideas for the lore behind the world. Which can be really good... if that's what your game wants, and you take it all with a grain of salt as you convert all of it to 2e. If you're not interested in playing those kinds of stories, more power to your group. If you're not, then the books are less than useful, and you'd be better served saving your money for the supplements already in the works coming out.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-10T05:47:03-08:00","id":783395,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kalamaris</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/695478-what-supplements-do-you-recommend?p=782753#post782753\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thanks for the reply. You see I'm in the (un)fortunate position to have many of them already.<br /> I own the Invictus, Ordo Dracul, Carthians, Lansea Sanctum, Circle of the Crone and VII books.<br /> Never got to read them before but they sit in my bookshelf patiently and I was planning to get to them after reading VtR 2nd ed. (order is on the way).<br /> <br /> I'm more interested in the fluff on the Covenants but I don't want to read obsolete material, so which of the above are the less affected by 2nd ed. changes fluff wise?</div> </div> </div> </div> I'd wait for Secrets of the Covenant to come out if you want to avoid any obsolete material. All the books you listed are from before the shift in direction of Vampire, and are likely outdated. I would say that pretty much anything from before Requiem for Rome came out should be considered obsolete in terms of fluff.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 12-10-2015, 08:49 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-10T09:22:05-08:00","id":783473,"title":"","content":"There's a lot of good suggestions here. I'm going to list <b>Night Horrors: Wicked Dead</b> as another one.<br /> <br /> <b>Vampire</b> wasn't my introduction to the <b>WoD</b>, and really I thought it was cliche and pretty bland compared to the other games. This supplement changed my mind because it cracked open the setting and revealed these other supernatural mysteries that exist in the night, too. It showed me how the world of <b>Vampire</b> wasn't just a world of political betrayal, but that it was <i>alive</i> and it <i>hungered</i> and going down the street at night <i>you weren't safe</i>.<br /> <br /> That, at night, even one of the Kindred weren't safe from that either","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}